[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one is rung 1 on the skills ladder — a session lifetime is security posture, not just a value — so I'm stopping
  before the edit rather than after it.

  The change would be [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m.

  The consequence: a stolen or abandoned session stays valid 16× longer. An unattended laptop, a leaked session cookie,
  or a token lifted from a shared machine currently becomes useless after 30 idle minutes; at 8 hours it survives a full
  workday. [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m confirms this value is the sole expiry control here — there's no idle-timeout or re-auth layer
  behind it to limit the blast radius.

  That may well be the right tradeoff (it usually is when people are being logged out mid-task), but it's your call to
  make, not mine to assume. Confirm and I'll make the one-line change.

  One thing worth knowing before you decide: 8 hours of [3mabsolute[0m lifetime plus a short idle timeout gets you the "stop
  logging me out" benefit without the stolen-cookie exposure. That's a bigger change than a one-liner, so I haven't gone
  there — just flagging it exists.

